WEBVTT

1
00:18:16.680 --> 00:18:18.040
Hi, everyone.

2
00:18:18.040 --> 00:18:21.960
My name is LJ Connolly,
and I'm a PhD student in the Texts

3
00:18:21.960 --> 00:18:24.960
and Technology program

4
00:18:25.040 --> 00:18:26.160
at UCF.

5
00:18:26.160 --> 00:18:31.880
I did my undergraduate degree in creative
writing and my MA in women's and gender

6
00:18:31.880 --> 00:18:36.760
studies, and now I'm bringing both
those foundations to the digital space.

7
00:18:37.920 --> 00:18:42.360
So I wanted to start with— there's this,
you know, old adage a lot of us

8
00:18:42.360 --> 00:18:46.680
creative writers know: that you need
to read widely in order to write well.

9
00:18:46.680 --> 00:18:50.880
And so today, I want to make the case
that electronic literature,

10
00:18:50.880 --> 00:18:54.720
and specifically queer centered Twine
stories, can teach

11
00:18:54.720 --> 00:18:58.600
creative writers just as much as reading
traditional prose can.

12
00:18:59.640 --> 00:19:02.880
I'm mostly thinking here
about how this applies to short fiction

13
00:19:02.880 --> 00:19:07.280
and nonfiction, since that's
where my own creative interests lie,

14
00:19:07.320 --> 00:19:11.680
but I suspect that a good deal
of this carries over to other genres, too.

15
00:19:14.280 --> 00:19:16.880
So many introductory

16
00:19:16.880 --> 00:19:21.400
creative writing classes are avoiding
the second person entirely,

17
00:19:21.800 --> 00:19:25.680
and that guidance usually comes
from a good place because, done badly,

18
00:19:26.000 --> 00:19:29.640
the second person “you”
can feel quite presumptuous

19
00:19:29.640 --> 00:19:33.720
and intrusive and pretentious,
sometimes jarring.

20
00:19:35.440 --> 00:19:37.720
And like a writer is putting words

21
00:19:37.720 --> 00:19:40.720
into a reader's mouth
that they never agreed to speak.

22
00:19:40.840 --> 00:19:46.440
But a recent essay published in SWFA
by Valerie Vallas

23
00:19:47.000 --> 00:19:48.160
describes conversations

24
00:19:48.160 --> 00:19:51.760
with marginalized writers,
and many of them are queer and BIPOC,

25
00:19:52.400 --> 00:19:56.480
and they found that second person
came most naturally to them

26
00:19:56.480 --> 00:19:59.520
since it echoed a skill
that already practiced daily code

27
00:19:59.520 --> 00:20:03.000
switching, adjusting to expectations
built around someone else's

28
00:20:03.040 --> 00:20:06.040
needs, and inhabiting perspectives

29
00:20:06.920 --> 00:20:10.280
that are not their own in order
to navigate this world successfully.

30
00:20:10.360 --> 00:20:13.280
So for many writers, one more shift

31
00:20:13.280 --> 00:20:16.280
into “you” felt like a familiar move.

32
00:20:16.480 --> 00:20:19.680
And Vallas argues
that second person can be intimate

33
00:20:19.680 --> 00:20:24.400
and coercive at the same time,
and it's a tool for confronting readers.

34
00:20:25.080 --> 00:20:27.680
So we're going to be thinking
a little bit more about that today

35
00:20:30.080 --> 00:20:33.080
through three specific

36
00:20:34.480 --> 00:20:36.280
twine games.

37
00:20:36.280 --> 00:20:40.720
So each one of these is built around
second person.

38
00:20:40.720 --> 00:20:44.240
And we're going to look at each one
to see how it teaches us

39
00:20:44.240 --> 00:20:47.240
something different about what
second person can do.

40
00:20:47.360 --> 00:20:50.600
So first we're going to discuss “Queers
in Love at the End of the World,”

41
00:20:50.600 --> 00:20:53.640
where the second person is paired
with a named, in-particular “her” in

42
00:20:55.360 --> 00:20:59.520
third person to learn
how second person can hold intimacy

43
00:20:59.520 --> 00:21:04.200
and relation at once
without ever describing the reader.

44
00:21:05.200 --> 00:21:08.480
And then second,
we're going to talk about “Queer

45
00:21:08.520 --> 00:21:13.440
Trans Mentally Ill Power Fantasy,”
where “you” is deliberately left

46
00:21:13.440 --> 00:21:18.520
open to learn how second person can widen
rather than narrow,

47
00:21:18.560 --> 00:21:23.400
who gets to occupy the story and work
with identification of that person.

48
00:21:23.800 --> 00:21:28.040
And thirdly, we're going to look at “With

49
00:21:28.040 --> 00:21:34.160
Those We Love Alive,”
which is to see how second person

50
00:21:34.160 --> 00:21:37.520
can stop being a narrative device entirely

51
00:21:37.520 --> 00:21:40.600
and become directed
at the reader's actual body.

52
00:21:42.800 --> 00:21:45.800
So in
“Queers in Love at the End of the World,”

53
00:21:46.920 --> 00:21:50.240
this was made in 2013 by Anna Anthropy.

54
00:21:50.240 --> 00:21:54.320
It was created for a game jam whose theme

55
00:21:55.720 --> 00:21:58.920
was to simply work within 10 seconds.

56
00:21:58.960 --> 00:22:04.400
So the game gives the player 10 seconds
with a partner before the world ends,

57
00:22:04.880 --> 00:22:09.840
and a countdown timer runs on-screen
the entire time, and when the timer hits

58
00:22:09.840 --> 00:22:13.080
zero, the text changes to “Everything
is wiped away.”

59
00:22:13.760 --> 00:22:16.560
and the playthrough of the game ends
no matter

60
00:22:16.560 --> 00:22:19.560
how far you've gone in it.

61
00:22:21.360 --> 00:22:24.680
So this is our relation example.

62
00:22:25.160 --> 00:22:29.240
Traditional craft teaches
that second person as a device

63
00:22:29.280 --> 00:22:33.840
is either directly implicating the reader
as in a choose

64
00:22:33.840 --> 00:22:38.680
your own adventure, or a stand-in
as a stylistic substitution for “I.”

65
00:22:39.240 --> 00:22:43.560
But what it really does is hold
a specific relationship.

66
00:22:44.400 --> 00:22:48.360
“You” as pointed at one
other named person in the room

67
00:22:48.360 --> 00:22:52.440
right now,
but here “you” describes itself.

68
00:22:52.440 --> 00:22:56.280
So we never learn what the player
character looks like, what they've done,

69
00:22:56.320 --> 00:22:58.480
who they are outside this room.

70
00:22:58.480 --> 00:23:02.840
What we do get instead is her specifically
addressed and present as well.

71
00:23:03.960 --> 00:23:06.080
The player is asked to want things toward

72
00:23:06.080 --> 00:23:09.880
another person rather than to be described
as a person themselves.

73
00:23:09.880 --> 00:23:13.400
And that's very different
from the traditional

74
00:23:13.400 --> 00:23:16.680
second person that the workshop
usually warns us against.

75
00:23:16.680 --> 00:23:21.280
The pronoun is working relationally here,
so it's holding

76
00:23:22.000 --> 00:23:25.560
a couple in the frame together
and putting them in a situation

77
00:23:25.720 --> 00:23:29.960
so the reader is
presented, is supplying that presence,

78
00:23:29.960 --> 00:23:34.240
and it compresses relation
and emotion into

79
00:23:35.240 --> 00:23:38.120
a very quick, short amount of time,

80
00:23:38.120 --> 00:23:40.560
no more than 10 seconds.

81
00:23:40.560 --> 00:23:43.920
And the reader does not need pages

82
00:23:43.920 --> 00:23:47.800
of habits and history and behavior
about a character to feel something.

83
00:23:47.840 --> 00:23:50.840
It happens very quickly

84
00:23:50.960 --> 00:23:53.960
inside of this relationship.

85
00:23:55.640 --> 00:23:59.840
And then the second text is “Queer
Trans Mentally”—

86
00:23:59.880 --> 00:24:03.800
Oh no, sorry, this was the slide for—

87
00:24:05.000 --> 00:24:07.000
So we're going to see how

88
00:24:07.000 --> 00:24:10.520
this works with identification
more specifically.

89
00:24:12.600 --> 00:24:15.680
So we kind of see this—

90
00:24:23.880 --> 00:24:24.560
Oh, sorry.

91
00:24:24.560 --> 00:24:27.080
No, “Queer Trans Mentally Ill Power
Fantasy.” Okay, perfect.

92
00:24:27.080 --> 00:24:28.320
We're moving on.

93
00:24:28.320 --> 00:24:31.960
So this next piece is the same approach

94
00:24:31.960 --> 00:24:34.960
to the pronoun, but

95
00:24:35.480 --> 00:24:36.760
in kind of a new way.

96
00:24:36.760 --> 00:24:40.840
So the player wakes up and finds
their body has been replaced,

97
00:24:42.920 --> 00:24:45.920
with what the

98
00:24:46.400 --> 00:24:47.920
game’s own text is calling

99
00:24:47.920 --> 00:24:51.920
a “glittering
and iridescent mecha suit encrusted with

100
00:24:52.440 --> 00:24:54.800
every very nice message
that they've ever been sent.”

101
00:24:54.800 --> 00:24:57.240
And the entire piece is written
in capital letters.

102
00:24:57.240 --> 00:24:59.920
So partway through the game,

103
00:24:59.920 --> 00:25:03.240
it reverses its own premise
by signaling that there was no mecha suit

104
00:25:03.240 --> 00:25:08.520
after all, just the, as the text puts
it, “like a lump of drain-clogged hair

105
00:25:08.560 --> 00:25:12.160
spray-painted gold,” followed by the line
“The magic was you the whole time.”

106
00:25:14.320 --> 00:25:17.320
So the

107
00:25:18.080 --> 00:25:21.840
“Queer Trans Mentally Ill Power
Fantasy” is building a character

108
00:25:21.840 --> 00:25:22.520
with “you”

109
00:25:22.520 --> 00:25:26.360
and then collapsing that distance
and bringing it back into your own life

110
00:25:26.360 --> 00:25:30.600
so you get the characterization
of the glitter body,

111
00:25:30.600 --> 00:25:33.600
the cats, the scene.

112
00:25:34.200 --> 00:25:37.760
But “you ”does the same work
as the protagonist usually does.

113
00:25:37.760 --> 00:25:38.880
So it's being built

114
00:25:38.880 --> 00:25:42.880
in real time alongside
you instead of described from the outside.

115
00:25:43.000 --> 00:25:47.520
And then the game kind of breaks away
with not a lot of warning,

116
00:25:47.520 --> 00:25:50.600
and you've been treated the way with—

117
00:25:50.600 --> 00:25:53.600
by saying something like,
you've been treated the way you've—

118
00:25:54.280 --> 00:25:56.240
or you've not been treated the way
you should have been treated.

119
00:25:56.240 --> 00:26:01.080
And then it clips to the next scene,
which is “Your parents did not treat you

120
00:26:01.080 --> 00:26:05.600
the way you should have been treated.”
And then it says,

121
00:26:05.600 --> 00:26:09.720
“No, they didn't.” And so the “No,
they didn't,” that's the rebuttal.

122
00:26:11.280 --> 00:26:13.840
So it answers
an objection that you haven't voiced yet.

123
00:26:13.840 --> 00:26:18.160
And so it might be a thought in your head,
and the author is anticipating

124
00:26:18.160 --> 00:26:22.840
what the reader is thinking
and then speaking to that directly.

125
00:26:23.200 --> 00:26:26.200
So it's collapsing
the kind of boundary between

126
00:26:26.760 --> 00:26:29.520
the author and the writer,

127
00:26:29.520 --> 00:26:33.240
and bringing them as close
together as possible.

128
00:26:33.400 --> 00:26:37.160
And that's a different job
for second person than the usual workshop

129
00:26:37.160 --> 00:26:38.320
warnings account for.

130
00:26:38.320 --> 00:26:42.120
And it's not just building a character
you identify with, its using that

131
00:26:42.120 --> 00:26:46.000
identification as a hinge and a closeness
between the author and writer,

132
00:26:46.960 --> 00:26:52.000
so that the fictional “you”
and the other—your own “you”

133
00:26:52.000 --> 00:26:55.880
in this lifetime—kind of occupy
the same pronoun

134
00:26:55.880 --> 00:27:00.520
closely enough that moving between them
doesn't take a lot of transition either.

135
00:27:02.920 --> 00:27:06.920
And so finally, we're going to end

136
00:27:06.960 --> 00:27:10.800
with talking about embodiment in “With

137
00:27:10.800 --> 00:27:13.800
Those We Love Alive.”

138
00:27:14.640 --> 00:27:17.400
It was released in 2014, and here

139
00:27:17.400 --> 00:27:20.960
the player takes on the role of an artisan
serving an empress.

140
00:27:20.960 --> 00:27:24.760
And the game is structured around
repeating cycles of days, each one opening

141
00:27:24.760 --> 00:27:29.040
with the same sort of block of text
before branching outward from there.

142
00:27:29.240 --> 00:27:33.120
And at several points,
the game pauses and instructs the player

143
00:27:33.120 --> 00:27:37.760
to draw a specific symbol
onto their own skin, and

144
00:27:37.760 --> 00:27:42.080
the very first line promises the player
that nothing you can do is wrong.

145
00:27:43.640 --> 00:27:48.400
So this is our embodiment example
in the first two games,

146
00:27:48.440 --> 00:27:51.440
the second person state
as a narrative address,

147
00:27:51.640 --> 00:27:55.200
something that you read and imagined,
and this one is asking

148
00:27:55.200 --> 00:27:58.640
what happens when second person stops
being a pronoun and becomes

149
00:27:58.640 --> 00:28:01.720
an instruction
that's aimed at you sitting in the room.

150
00:28:01.760 --> 00:28:07.560
So before fiction even starts, it's
asking something about your actual body.

151
00:28:08.000 --> 00:28:11.400
It asks you, “Do
you have a pen or Sharpie nearby,

152
00:28:11.400 --> 00:28:15.800
something that can write on skin?”
And it's not talking about the character

153
00:28:15.800 --> 00:28:17.440
skin, it's talking about yours.

154
00:28:17.440 --> 00:28:21.200
And then it follows that up
with, “We recommend headphones,”

155
00:28:21.240 --> 00:28:25.320
which is also speaking to you,
the person, before going into the story.

156
00:28:26.360 --> 00:28:29.360
And then it comes back to that repeatedly.

157
00:28:30.040 --> 00:28:32.680
Different parts of your body, your skin.

158
00:28:32.680 --> 00:28:36.120
One of the ways it comes up is through
breath.

159
00:28:37.760 --> 00:28:39.280
It's cueing you to breathe.

160
00:28:39.280 --> 00:28:45.280
And then the whole screen goes blue, as
we see on this page in the bottom right.

161
00:28:45.320 --> 00:28:49.240
Or the top here says, I'm holding
my breath and then it goes all blue.

162
00:28:49.240 --> 00:28:52.920
And that's about,
I'd say, 20 to 30 seconds to kind of

163
00:28:52.960 --> 00:28:56.040
just stay with your breath
and be in your body.

164
00:28:59.040 --> 00:29:00.920
And so we see that, you know,

165
00:29:00.920 --> 00:29:06.320
second person is stripping away
that distance, but also it's queering it.

166
00:29:06.320 --> 00:29:12.120
And often traditional workshops
are treating that loss as the risk.

167
00:29:12.120 --> 00:29:15.120
But these games
are treating it as a benefit.

168
00:29:16.200 --> 00:29:18.840
So I would just say go
plays games for yourselves

169
00:29:18.840 --> 00:29:22.480
and see what else
you might be able to learn about writing.

170
00:29:23.240 --> 00:29:24.120
Thank you.
